tests.numbers:
Answer to ask 2 and to the Board's item 5. Under Do Nothing the MAX delivers, against 684 a year of capacity:
- 2050: 513, or 42.8 a month (75% used);
- 2052: 425, or 35.4 (62%);
- 2053: 388, or 32.3 (57%);
- 2055: 346, or 28.8 (51%);
- 2057: 305, or 25.4 (45%);
- 2060: 248, or 20.7 (36%).
Idle slots run from 171 a year (2050) to 436 (2060). The waiting backlog is 0, so Albaugh's answer to a slowdown, burning down backlog instead of cutting rate [B-0584], is not available. The build rate has to follow demand down.

The steepest fall is behind us: 684 to 543 in 2047-48. That was -141 a year, about 12 a month, more than two steps in one year, and the brief reports no live problem after it. The steepest fall ahead is 2051-52: -78, or 6.5 a month. After 2053 the fall is 14-26 a year (1-2 a month).

A step-down ladder (inference; steps of 5 at the doctrine's spacing):
- 42 a month in 2050-51;
- 37 in 2052, then 32 six months later;
- 32 to 2055;
- 27 from about 2056;
- 22 from about 2058.
Over 2050-60 it builds 4,014 aircraft against demand of 3,962. The running gap stays between -23 and +53 aircraft, under two months of output, which delivery scheduling absorbs. That is four steps in about six years, never a cliff.

Each step is taken on the same six KPIs and announced to the supply chain within the planning window, as the 787 went from 14 to 12 to 10 a month in 2020-21 [B-1614, B-1641]. If a KPI degrades on a step, we hold that rate and fix it before the next step.

Lines (inference, at about 15.7 a month per Renton line): we have four today. Three carry 42 a month (89% loaded), and two carry 27 (86%) from about 2056. Consolidate one line at a time, on the gates; which line goes is a KPI call, not a date.

Doctrine's cautions: Boeing's bias is toward stability [B-1588, B-0659], and rate cuts spread fixed cost over fewer airplanes [B-0239]. Operating profit already runs $0.66-1.43B a year below the game baseline. Honest delivery dates throughout [BX-1274, BX-1310].

This is an other move; the board does not price it.

Answers to ask 4 and to the Board's item 4.

The board's risk case costs the fps rows little. S3 against S1 is -$0.06B on every 10-year and via Embraer row, and -$0.08B to -$0.09B on every 7-year row. Little rides on the nominal date because there is no positive value to protect. Against Do Nothing:
- 10-year at Renton: -0.86 / -0.85 / -0.92;
- best fps row (10-year at Renton + Re-engine): -0.56 / -1.60 / -0.62;
- 7-year at Renton: -1.73 / -1.73 / -1.81.

The board does not price doctrine's clean-sheet slip of +3 years central (range +2 to +5). On that slip, a 2060 EIS becomes 2063:
- the long-run split against NGSA 2037 moves from 27/73 to 24/76;
- rate 50 moves from about 2064 to 2067.
Certification is the pacing item [B-2343]. Our last clean sheet entered service in 2011 after repeated re-plans [B-0526]. By 2050 we will have run no major development for 23 years, and 33 by a 2060 EIS.

What kind of failure it is:
- not a KPI failure: nothing is live;
- not a supplier failure: no problem is live, LEAP-1B is easing, and the Joint Venture and CFM engines are mature;
- not a strain failure: a lone fps overlaps nothing.
It fails on value at the nominal date and on unpriced predictability: certification pacing, and an engineering workforce out of practice. This is my flag against any launch this round (ask 10).

Were the CEO to launch, the public EIS would carry the slip range beside the nominal date (2060, range 2062-65). Before a launch, no date [B-2315].

Answer to ask 8: operations backs the 10-year.

10-year:
- EIS 2060 on a 2050 launch;
- $60.32B over 2050-59, peaking at about $8.44B (14%) in about 2056;
- stress net debt $0.0B in all four 10-year rows;
- strain with a same-round Re-engine: 2 years ($1.2B), legal.

7-year:
- EIS 2057;
- $65.00B over 2050-56, about $9.29B a year;
- stress peaks after 2050 of $1.0-1.1B at Renton and $2.6-2.7B at the new site;
- a buffer margin of only $0.05-0.19B;
- strain with the Re-engine: 5 years, barred.

The 10-year leads by $0.87-0.93B alone in every column (a tie the smaller bill wins) and by $1.00-1.06B with the Re-engine. Its worst column is higher: -1.78 against -2.66 at Renton alone, and -2.53 against -3.53 with the Re-engine. The 7-year's 3-year lead buys 3 points of long-run split (30/70 against 27/73), and the grid already nets that.

Do Nothing:
- no programme cash;
- base FCF $13.27B (2050), $12.76B (2055) and $12.66B (2060); stress about $8.1-8.7B (inference: two-thirds of $13.79B, less 79% of the profit erosion);
- net debt $0.0B in base and stress to 2050;
- profit against the game baseline -$0.66B (2050), -$1.30B (2055) and -$1.43B (2060).

Re-engine:
- $5B over 2050-54, about $1.0B a year, or $0.79B after the 79% factor: 6% of base and 9% of stress FCF;
- net debt held at $0.0B in every column;
- undiscounted penalty $0.6B, for scale only (the grid already nets it).
Return: 0.44 - 1.04p at odds p of an A350 Re-engine, which is +0.08 at 35%, with break-even at 42%. It converts, but thinly: inside ε at any odds.

10-year:
- $60.32B over 2050-59, $47.65B in all after the 79% factor;
- a peak of about $8.44B (14%) in about 2056, or $6.67B after the factor: 52% of 2055 base FCF and about 82% of stress FCF;
- net debt holds ($0.0B stress peak), but the item is -0.85 to -1.02 against hold, and -0.56 to -1.70 against Do Nothing with the Re-engine.

7-year:
- $65.00B over 2050-56, about $9.29B a year ($7.34B after the factor, about 90% of stress FCF);
- stress peaks of $1.0-6.4B after 2050;
- -1.56 to -2.74 against Do Nothing.

Via Embraer:
- $100B, with a stress peak of $6.0-12.4B;
- -3.12 to -4.26 against Do Nothing.

Undiscounted penalties, for scale only: $19.1B (10-year), $22.0B (7-year) and $52.5B (via Embraer). The grid already nets them.

The cash goes out and the value does not come back: the best fps alone returns -$0.86B.

