recommendations.text:
The CFO should show the cash case:
- with and without the Re-engine: $1.0B a year in 2050-54, no strain, $4.23B of headroom under $5.228B;
- a net-cash path at payouts of 30% and 50%;
- any buyback inside the 18-month AGM authority, sized after programme spend, as a separate shareholder-policy item.
No equity raise. State support and refundable advances count as upside only.

Add four items to the full Board's twice-yearly safety review:
- NGSA at full rate on two engine sources;
- the Joint Venture engine's in-service durability, by operating environment, hot and sandy included, as Pratt & Whitney says it will report it;
- any Rolls-Royce content entering that engine only as an engineering change with its own test evidence;
- support for the in-service A320 fleet now that the lines are closed, and the A350's single widebody engine source (Trent XWB).
A quality escape, a safety event or an engine supply crunch comes back to the Board at once.

Objectives, for the record:
- 60/40: met in 2045 (66.0%) and 2050 (72.3%), but lost in 2040 (49.2%);
- protect the A320 family: lost in 2035 (58.7%);
- stable engine supply: met (2 sources, 3 makers).
No order this round changes any of them. The Board trades no safety and no PV for them.

