frame.asks_ops:
Lars, please bring the production, supply and quality memo for the last round.

(1) The four results the Board never lets me override, for NGSA at full rate (1,339 in 2050, capacity 1,366 from 2053) and for the in-service A320 fleet after its lines closed in 2047:
- safety;
- quality;
- the ramp shield, now meaning in-service A320 support and sustaining engineering kept staffed;
- supply crunch.
Give pass or fail on the default row and on the Re-engine row.

(2) Supply.
- The NGSA load is 2,678-2,732 engines a year on CFM and the Joint Venture, below the 2,770 we carried in 2044. Is any engine, structures, titanium or rare-earth constraint live?
- Is the Joint Venture engine's durability by operating environment a watch item or a constraint?
- If there is a crunch, doctrine adds no new concurrency and the Re-engine waits.

(3) Engineering capacity for an A350 Re-engine developed in 2050-55, alongside NGSA in-service support. Confirm rule 6: zero overlap, zero strain, one launch.

(4) The Board's question: has anything changed on a mature widebody engine for the A350 since 2045? This is a five-pillars question first.
On the record:
- no UltraFan widebody and no A350 Trent upgrade is committed;
- Rolls-Royce goes to its board only in the round a Re-engine is on the record, and no later round exists;
- an UltraFan widebody launched blind this round would be ready in 2056, a year after a 2055 EIS;
- the GEnx upgrade is a 787 engine and the Trent 1000 upgrade is too; the A350 flies only the Trent XWB [A-0253].
Tell me:
- which engine a 2055 Re-engine would carry, and whether it would be mature;
- if none would be, whether the EIS must move;
- whether this is a five-pillars flag or a re-timing note;
- whether your readiness soft veto stands.

(5) A350F and Spirit A350 absorption, shown by a milestone: 70 a year in each year 2026-2049, with no event. State the limit of that evidence.

(6) Production readiness on the A350 lines for a Re-engine and its ramp.

(7) Five pillars on the other moves.
- Confirm for the ECSC that the supply agreements and staffing are sized to our own production and aimed at no rival.
- Redraft the public staffing text with the clause 'it is not a Delay Tactics move' struck, as the Board asks.
- Confirm that Delay Tactics are deleted on integrity and rule 4.

(8) Draft the Board's four items for its twice-yearly safety review:
- NGSA at full rate on two engine sources;
- the Joint Venture engine's in-service durability by operating environment, hot and sandy included;
- Rolls-Royce content only as an engineering change with its own test evidence;
- in-service A320 fleet support, plus the A350's single widebody engine source (Trent XWB).
A quality escape, a safety event or a supply crunch comes back to the Board at once.

(9) Your pass or fail by row, and whether any failure is binding.

tests.numbers:
Method (an inference, unchanged from rounds 2-3):
- FCF before programmes = $5.228B + 0.667 x (OP - 3.6);
- net income = $5.743B + 0.732 x (OP - 3.6);
- dividend = payout x net income.
Start from end-2049: $80.5B at 48%, $123.7B at 30%, $75.7B at 50%. Operating profit from the brief: 20.0 / 20.2 / 20.1 / 19.4 / 19.2.
- FCF: 16.17 / 16.30 / 16.23 / 15.77 / 15.63, $80.1B in total.
- Net income: 17.75 / 17.89 / 17.82 / 17.31 / 17.16.
- The Re-engine adds no operating profit before its 2055 EIS.

Net cash at end-2054, without / with the Re-engine:
- 30%: $177.4B / $172.4B, path 134.5 / 145.5 / 156.4 / 166.9 / 177.4 without;
- 48% practice: $118.4B / $113.4B, path 88.1 / 95.9 / 103.5 / 111.0 / 118.4 without;
- 50%: $111.8B / $106.8B, path 83.0 / 90.3 / 97.7 / 104.8 / 111.8 without.
The Re-engine costs $5.0B of net cash, 6.2% of 2050-54 FCF.

Buyback, sized after programme spend (the yearly surplus = FCF - Re-engine - dividend):
- 48%: about $7.6B a year without the Re-engine and $6.6B with it, or $11.4B / $9.9B per 18-month authority;
- 50%: $7.2B / $6.2B a year, or $10.8B / $9.3B per authority;
- 30%: $10.7B / $9.7B a year, or $16.1B / $14.6B per authority.
The authority caps holdings at 10% of issued capital. Using the board's $150B enterprise value as a proxy for market value (an inference), that is about $15B. At 48-50% the surplus fits. At 30% without the Re-engine it exceeds the cap, and the excess stays as net cash.
Any buyback is a separate shareholder-policy Board item.

No equity raise in any case. State support and refundable advances count as upside only.

There is no fps EIS to spread over, so each $1B falls in 2050 on the board's booking. Peak spend would be:
- $1.0B (one move, hold);
- $2.0B (one move with the Re-engine);
- $3.0B (both moves with the Re-engine).
All are inside $5.228B. The cash item that decides is the $48.32B fine (see the fine-exposure row).

(1) Default (ngsa hold, rea350 hold, delay_tactics none, a320_plan alt): passes every test.

(2) Re-engine row. It passes:
- peak cash ($1.0B a year, $4.23B of headroom);
- rule 6 (zero overlap);
- the row test (worst column +13.64);
- the -$2B line (-0.42);
- rule 3 (open today);
- net cash.
It fails the added-move test (+0.57 at most, -0.42 in S6 / S7). That is a binding soft veto, not a red line, and no override can carry it.

(3) Every Delay Tactics row breaks red lines (rules 4 and 5; 'both' is also a second move), integrity for bottleneck, and the -$2B line. Deleted.

No quality escape is reported.

Statement flags:
- windows, not dates;
- no engine named for any A350 development;
- no Re-engine signal unless the Board approves one;
- no Delay Tactics, so the 2045 staffing clause is struck;
- no payout figures.
NGSA's two engines (CFM, and the P&W-Rolls-Royce Joint Venture) are mature and in service, so they can be named.

The CEO's draft public line passes on these terms, on both rows.

No crunch is reported for engines, structures, titanium or rare earths.

Engine load at two engines per aircraft:
- 2050: 2,678;
- 2051: 2,704;
- 2052: 2,688;
- 2053: 2,596;
- 2054: 2,568;
- capped at 2,732 by capacity (1,366).
Every year is below the 2,770 we carried in 2044 (1,385 aircraft) with no crunch.

Sources: CFM plus the Joint Venture, so 2 sources from 3 makers. Titanium and rare earths stay at or below the 2044 peak.

The Joint Venture's durability by operating environment, hot and sandy included, is a watch item, not a constraint. P&W has said it will report the data, and no adverse finding is reported. It becomes a constraint only if the data show a time-on-wing or shop-capacity shortfall.

The trigger does not fire, so it does not block the Re-engine. The missing widebody engine is a readiness problem, not a crunch.

