Confirm all of these are identical in S1 and S2, and confirm Lobby alone at about -1.00 (inference; no row).

(2) Strain, re-tied on this grid. Last round your model tied with no new strain. My check, on your model (inference):
- CFM's 26.98 pp on the NGSA at about $0.22B per pp from 2050 to 2056 is $5.97B, plus $8B, which gives -13.97;
- 19.04 pp is $4.21B, plus $9B, which gives -13.22.
Confirm the bills are $8B and $9B, not $11B, and label it inference.

(3) For each option against hold, give its value in the expected column (S1), its cost in S1, its worst column and the premium in dollars. My read:
- hold $0;
- Open Fan $13.97B;
- Open Fan + Lobby $13.22B.
Both now sit inside the $15B RISE allowance. Tell me plainly whether any financial case uses it. My read is none: it buys no share inside the window, no objective, and no value the board counts after 2056. The allowance is a ceiling, not a budget. Confirm the game total ends at $0 of $4.0B.

(4) Weights, provisional .75 / .25. Challenge them if you see it differently. Nothing turns on them, because every row is identical across the columns.

(5) Reconciliation. Round 3 forecast -28.04 for S1 and today's row is -28.04. The 2045-49 shares (52.2 / 51.8 / 51.7 / 48.8 / 46.7) and engines (2,106 / 2,083 / 2,089 / 1,772 / 1,671) match round 3's table. Confirm, and state the book-to-row gap (-30.15 against -28.04) as the Embraer rule, not an error.

(6) Embraer. Confirm that no airframe, volume or spend is agreed, and re-tie its in-row value (+2.11 last round). I'll keep the booking rule consistent: S1 less Embraer, about -30.15.

(7) Floor. Hold adds no new R&D or strain. Confirm the 70% payout ($5.95B on about $8.5B) is whole and that there is no rating risk (debt $35B against EV $160B). For the record: the Open Fan is $0.8B a year, and Open Fan + Lobby $0.9B ($1.1B with strain), against about $2.55B of room.

(8) Terms and volumes.
- Airbus has put on the public record an extension of the NGSA engine slots at 2,612-2,732 engines a year across CFM and the Joint Venture (about 1,306-1,366 aircraft). At about 27% that would be roughly 705-737 CFM engines a year (my inference).
