- risk, investment and revenue shared, one engine;
- the gear and core P&W's work-share and IP;
- contributions recognised only in the split, with no licence and no cash to the partner.
A private move records that any reopening of the equal split (26.7% each in 2049), or any departure from the terms, is a significant transaction through Finance to the full Board before signature. No reopening is sought.

board_guidance.expect_to_see:
Before we review, the package should contain the following.

(1) Grid rows. The bulletin rules out none of the four columns, so all are plausible.
- Hold row, 'UltraFan NB: hold': +11.03 / +11.03 / +11.03 / +11.37 (S1-S4).
- For any Board item, its row against hold, column by column:
  - Trent 1000 upgrade: 'UltraFan NB: hold + T1000 upgrade' (+4.30 / +4.30 / +4.30 / +4.75) less hold = -6.73 / -6.73 / -6.73 / -6.62.
  - UltraFan WB: 'UltraFan NB: hold + UltraFan WB' (+2.03 / +2.03 / +2.35 / +3.05) less hold = -9.00 / -9.00 / -8.68 / -8.32.
- Our reading (inference: row gain plus bill plus the $5B strain): the upgrade buys about $0.27B of engine value ($0.38B in S4); UltraFan WB buys $0 / $0 / $0.32B / $0.68B. To stay within its own bill, each would need $5B.
- McCabe's 2045 estimate for 2050 (about -6.7 and -8.6) is confirmed.
- If every field is at hold there is no Board item. We still want these rows shown, with the reason each item is held.

(2) The CEO's expected scenario, `predictions` and weights.
- Our illustrative weights are 0.80 / 0.05 / 0.10 / 0.05, and S1 is most likely:
  - Boeing launches no new airplane this year and gives no date.
  - Its 787 work with GE is 'not a 787 Re-engine order'.
  - Airbus keeps the A350 family as its widebody focus.
- On these weights hold is +11.05, the upgrade -6.72 and UltraFan WB -8.93. A Re-engine stress (0.40 / 0.10 / 0.30 / 0.20) gives -6.71 and -8.77. Each item has the same sign in every column, so no verdict turns on the weights.

(3) From McCabe:
- her co-signature, or her withheld signature, on any launch;
- each item's worst column against its own bill: the upgrade -6.73 against -$2B, UltraFan WB -9.00 against -$4B;
- capex a year if launched: about $0.67B (about £0.49bn) for either item, the upgrade over 2050-52 and UltraFan WB over 2050-55;
- confirmation that no balance-sheet veto is invoked, and that the payout and buyback cadence are unchanged;
- the final Joint Venture lookback for the Audit Committee, updated to the 2050 brief: $5.71B against the $4B line, delivered share 26.4% in 2050, and the return against the mid-to-high-teens hurdle.

(4) From Watson:
- the Joint Venture engine's in-service record on NGSA against the written standard: time on wing, shop-visit rate, life-limited parts, aircraft on ground;
- the support network scaled to about 8,848 Rolls-Royce engines by the end of 2050;
- the engineering-change rule;
- the Trent XWB and Trent 7000 ring-fence;
- for any widebody launch, its count or ready year: the upgrade counts from 2053; UltraFan WB is ready in 2056 and counts only with a re-engined 787 or A350 in service (earliest EIS 2055 in S3-S4), so it earns nothing in S1-S2.

(5) Both ExCo veto checks. Every red-line flag should be struck or rebutted in `rationale`, and `overrides` should be empty.

(6) `premium_b` at 0, or any widebody shortfall declared item by item with its reason.

(7) The public statement:
- no dates;
- nothing on Airbus's or Boeing's dates or rates;
- no claim of a share gain;
- any widebody line kept conditional on a Re-engine on the record, on Board approval and on paying after the cost of running two programmes.

(8) An objective note:
- narrowbody entry met;
- widebody at 55.2% (58.0% in S4);
- the $4B line not met at $5.71B;
- the strain at $0 only while both widebody fields stay at hold.

(9) A `board_response` to each round-3 recommendation, with a final status, since this is the last round.

would_approve:
All four fields at hold:
- `ultrafan_nb_solo: hold`, the only legal value; the Solo is folded;
- `jv_with_pw: hold`, which keeps the formed Joint Venture as approved;
- `ultrafan_wb: hold`;
- `t1000_upgrade: hold`.
These are not Board items, and we never veto a hold. The hold row is +11.03 / +11.03 / +11.03 / +11.37 (S1-S4), or +11.05 on our weights, and it is the best grid plan in every column. Strain stays at $0, and widebody share stays at 55.2% in 2050 (58.0% in S4).

would_veto:
Any Board item on governance or safety grounds, if any of these apply:
- McCabe's co-signature is withheld or absent;
- a binding ExCo veto goes unrespected, or a red-line flag is left unresolved;
- `overrides` is not empty;
- Watson's binding Civil veto still stands on the final orders. Examples: a disclosed upgrade count earlier than 2053, an UltraFan WB date earlier than 2056, or any date for Rolls-Royce content entering the Joint Venture engine.
Acceptable alternative: `hold`.

recommendations.text:
Product safety in service. Watson should report the Joint Venture engine's NGSA fleet to the Safety, Energy Transition & Tech Committee against the written standard:
- time on wing in the specification;
- shop-visit rate;
- life-limited parts at full life;
- aircraft on ground.
The fleet grows from about 7,870 Rolls-Royce engines at the end of 2049 to about 8,848 at the end of 2050 (brief §1 sums). Rolls-Royce content enters the certified engine only as an engineering change with its own test evidence, never to hold a date or win a campaign. If the in-service data tell us something we do not like, we say so first, with P&W.

Capital and audit. McCabe should confirm:
- no new programme spend this round, and no balance-sheet trigger;
- the 30-40% payout and the buyback cadence under the capital frame, with narrowbody cash booked as delivered.
Bring the final Joint Venture lookback to the Audit Committee, updated to the 2050 brief:
- $5.71B of our own narrowbody money against the $4B line;
- delivered share 26.4% in 2050;
- the return against the mid-to-high-teens hurdle, stated plainly whether or not it clears it.
The UltraFan technology position stays funded inside a broadly flat gross R&D envelope.

The public statement should:
- say the Joint Venture engine is in service and paced on durability;
- give no dates of any kind;
- say nothing on Airbus's or Boeing's dates or rates;
- claim no share gain;
- keep any widebody line conditional on a Re-engine on the record, on Board approval and on paying after the cost of running two programmes.
'We won't do anything not profitable' stays. Medium-term guidance goes through the Board before release.

levers_in_play:
Other moves (not priced by the board):
- Joint Venture governance;
- the fleet safety report against the written standard;
- support scaled to about 8,848 engines;
- the supplier and capacity plan with Trent XWB and Trent 7000 ring-fenced;
- Trent durability and UltraFan technology inside the existing budget;
- the Joint Venture engine on offer on standard terms;
- the final Joint Venture lookback to the Audit Committee.

tests.threshold:
Engine value an item needs (inference: the grid books R&D and strain undiscounted and engine value as PV at 2026):
(a) to break even against hold: bill plus the $5B strain;
(b) to stay within its own bill: the strain alone;
(c) to sit within the premium cap: bill plus strain less the cap.

tests.numbers:
Engine value bought (row gain + bill + $5B strain):
- upgrade: +0.27 / +0.27 / +0.27 / +0.38, for $7B of bill plus strain;
- UltraFan WB: 0 / 0 / +0.32 / +0.68, for $9B.
Engine value needed:
- upgrade: (a) $7B, (b) $5B, (c) $4B against the $3B cap;
- UltraFan WB: (a) $9B, (b) $5B, (c) $8B against the $1B sub-cap.
No configuration on this board passes, on any weights. The two items are exclusive, neither has a launch_if_selected form, and no later round exists to cancel. Even S4 alone gives −6.62 and −8.32.
My 2045 estimate for 2050:
- Upgrade: held. I said about −6.7; it is −6.73.
- UltraFan WB: my −8.6 was the Re-engine case (about $0.44B of value). The brief gives −8.68 in S3 and −8.32 in S4, and −9.00 in S1-S2 where it buys nothing. The expected figure of −8.93 is about 0.3 worse than my estimate, because S1-S2 carry most of the weight.
What moved since 2045:
- the upgrade, from −6.48 to −6.73 (a shorter window, 2053-2061);
- UltraFan WB in S3-S4, from −8.28/−8.02 to −8.68/−8.32;
- S2 now equals S1;
- the S4 hold row, from +11.28 to +11.37.

Rows, absolute:
- hold: worst +11.03 (S1-S3);
- hold + upgrade: worst +4.30 (S1-S3);
- hold + UltraFan WB: worst +2.03 (S1-S2).
Items against hold:
- upgrade: worst −6.73, beyond its −$2B bill;
- UltraFan WB: worst −9.00, beyond its −$4B bill.
The recommended package (all hold) orders no item, so its item downside is 0 in every column.
No cancel write-off is available in the final round: a launch now lands its full bill and the $5B strain. The Joint Venture is ready and the Solo is folded, so nothing can be cancelled. No launch_if_selected is on offer.

tests.threshold:
Mid-to-high teens (15-18%) on margin. Stylised (inference):
- spend: $1.14B a year over 2030-34 ($5.71B);
- inflow: Rolls-Royce narrowbody lifecycle value from brief §1 (2037-2056, the board's narrowbody count) × Civil margin of 16.6% (2024) or 24.9% (1H25).

tests.numbers:
Has anything moved? No. Brief §1 is identical to 2045, line for line: +16.74 / −4.00 / −1.71 = +11.03.
Money: $5.71B of our own narrowbody money against the $4B line. Missed, beyond repair.
Delivered share:
- 23.6% (2045) and 26.4% (2050), against 28.0% and 31.3% on the 2035 grid;
- 26.7% observed in 2049;
- about 7,870 Rolls-Royce engines delivered by end-2049, 8,848 by end-2050 and 14,607 by 2056.
Benchmark: the right 2035 benchmark is S4, P&W commits (+14.06). Against it the miss is −$3.03B, all engine value: the PV of the narrowbody stream is 17.29 against 20.34. Against S1 (+11.77) the miss is −0.74: the +2.29 fold saving less the 3.03.
Cause, on my reading: CFM's ducted engine and the Embraer partnership, with the equal-share rule holding us level with P&W (inference).
Return on the full $5.71B:
- IRR 9.1% at a 16.6% margin and 12.0% at 24.9%;
- NPV at 15% (2030 money) −2.41 / −1.42; at 18%, −2.90 / −2.24;
- breakeven margin 36.7% at 15% and 53.2% at 18%;
- undiscounted payback 2044-45.
Verdict: it adds value, but it does not clear the mid-to-high-teens hurdle.
Hold it as a floor: the board's 1.6x lifecycle multiple is narrower than our aftermarket (services worth more than 4x OE). Durability is a shared exposure with P&W, whose GTF fleet is 'still not where our customers need it to be'.
My record on engine-launch economics is low, and I have none on Joint Ventures.

tests.threshold:
The public statement and the public other moves must not:
- disclose an UltraFan WB date earlier than launch + 6, or an upgrade count earlier than launch + 3;
- give any date for Rolls-Royce content entering the Joint Venture engine;
- give any Joint Venture date other than the bulletin's (ready 2036; in service on NGSA since 2037);
- say anything about Airbus's or Boeing's dates or rates.
The CEO adds: no board grid numbers in public text.

other_moves.detail:
The governance package:
- one engineering interface;
- one durability standard and one fleet report, by operating environment, hot and sandy included, as P&W publishes;
- one chief engineer per change, with each change carrying its own test plan, certification basis and fleet-introduction gate, reported to the Committee;
- crediting as a joint technical valuation recorded at each gate, feeding the share split only: no licence and no cash;
- changes rationed, fleet first.
Any Joint Venture share or term question goes to the Board before it is public. This is company doctrine, not my record [R-0318, R-1010, RX-0072].

Watson reports the Rolls-Royce Joint Venture fleet (about 7,870 engines at end-2049, about 8,848 at end-2050) against the written standard: time on wing in the specification, shop-visit rate, life-limited parts at full life, and aircraft on ground. The brief carries no fleet data, so no figure is invented and neither compliance nor shortfall is claimed. If the data are bad, the order is:
1. operators and Airbus are told first, jointly with P&W;
2. then the public, with the fix and no recovery date we cannot hold;
3. spares and lease engines go to aircraft on ground first;
4. no engineering change is rushed in to cover it [RX-0296, RX-0283, RG-0049].

McCabe confirms the capital frame: no new programme spend (£0), no equity, no new borrowing and leverage unchanged. The payout stays at 30-40%, and buybacks are set on fit, value and timing, never on borrowed money. Narrowbody cash is booked as delivered.
The final lookback, stated plainly:
- $5.71B against the $4B line: missed, beyond repair;
- delivered share 23.6% (2045) and 26.4% (2050);
- IRR about 9.1-12.0% against the 15-18% hurdle: it adds value, but it does not clear the hurdle;
- against the 2035 benchmark (S4, +14.06), a $3.03B engine-value miss.
The IRR is a floor on the board's lifecycle multiple. Joint Venture aftermarket quality (LTSA balance and any onerous-contract test against the written durability standard) becomes a standing Audit Committee item on delivered numbers [RX-0213, R-1579, R-0900, RX-0242].

board_response.response:
Taken up in full, as a private governance move:
- one engineering interface;
- one durability standard and one fleet report;
- one chief engineer per change, with a fleet-introduction gate;
- crediting as a valuation in the share split only, with no licence and no cash;
- changes rationed, fleet first;
- partner shops added to P&W's network, not replacing it;
- any share or term question to the Board before it is public.
Durability is a shared exposure with P&W's GTF experience, and we treat it as such.
FINAL STATUS: met. Equal shares held (26.7% each in 2049), and no share or term question went public.

